_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":4,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I approve PKT-0048. The check does what the goal asks, nothing in the contract changed, and it stays within budget.\n\n**What I checked**\n- **Goal:** the upstream test page can't be used as a test suite. The executor showed that `test/documenteditor/main` has no assertions and needs the development scripts, so they wrote a Playwright check instead. It opens a new docx, waits for the toolbar and for the document to finish loading, types a marker and closes the editor. It then waits for the save and confirms the marker is inside the saved file's `word/document.xml`. It records each phase's time and the total, and reports PASS or FAIL with exit code 0 or 1.\n- **Atlas card:** the oracle level is now `partial` and its source points to the script. Choosing `partial` over `good` is fair, because the check does not test the open-order rules (sdk first, `asc_LoadDocument` after `onEditorPermissions`). The card says so.\n- **Contract and budget:** 5 files and about 79 lines changed (the limits are 6 and 800), all inside `target.files` and `.theseus/oracle`. No existing test or source file was touched.\n- **Assertions:** they are strong. The save is not judged from the file's modification time alone; the script also requires the typed marker, which is unique per run, to be in the saved file. That rules out passing on an unrelated rewrite. A server that is down produces FAIL.\n- **Packet record:** the Executor log is filled in. No follow-ups were asked for. The surprises in the log (the canvas click target, lint ignoring `.theseus/`, the test page not being usable) are about the test tooling, not about how the editor behaves, so they don't need to be kept as tests. They are recorded in the log and on the card. The Lessons section and the friction line are present.\n\n**Minor points that don't block approval**\n- The script reads the file's time just before closing the page. If the server saved early, during the 2 s pause after typing, the loop could wait for a second save that never comes and FAIL after the 90 s timeout. That would be a false failure, never a false pass.\n- The storage path and the global Playwright install are written into the script for this machine. Both are documented and can be overridden with an environment variable.\n- Each run leaves a `new (N).docx` file in the example app's storage, as the log already says.\n- The check is not one of the acceptance commands and runs against the build deployed under `/var/www`, not the checkout. Both limits are stated clearly on the card. Refactor packets will need to build and deploy before running it.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVE Headless open/type/save oracle checks the typed marker in the saved file; card set honestly to partial; within target and budget","ttft_ms":2540,"type":"result","duration_ms":15940,"uuid":"e42287a6-4f96-4950-832a-d37c419350a4","ttft_stream_ms":1016,"time_to_request_ms":295,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":8,"system_prompt":125,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":6,"input_attachments":24,"input_hooks":3,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":16,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":8,"init_frame_send":5,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":5,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":16,"query_setup":5,"auto_mode_context_wait":9,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":34,"message_normalization":3,"client_creation":8,"request_build":13},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":68,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":231,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":215,"process_anon_mib_turn_start":140,"process_rss_mib_result":227,"process_anon_mib_result":138,"process_peak_rss_mib":254,"process_cpu_ms_turn":1034,"process_wall_ms_turn":15939,"process_cpu_ms_streaming":358,"process_wall_ms_streaming":13190,"main_stream_count":4,"main_stream_event_count":260,"main_stream_chunk_count":90